  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Factors are less mobile across borders: labour and capital cannot move as freely as goods, which is what gives countries different cost structures.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    More output from the same resources — a productivity comparison. The opportunity-cost comparison is comparative advantage, which is a different question.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lower opportunity cost. Trade gains come from what each country gives up, not from how much it produces per worker.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rice: one bag costs Nigeria half a bale of cloth but Ghana a whole bale. Ghana's advantage is in cloth, where it gives up one bag of rice per bale against Nigeria's two.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ghana needs 3 h for a bale of cloth and 3 h for a bag of rice, so the cloth costs it exactly one bag of rice. The 3 is the input requirement, not the opportunity cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Comparative advantage. The relatively less efficient country is still least bad at something, and specialising there raises total output.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    World output rises through specialisation. Self-sufficiency and guaranteed price falls are the protectionist's claims, not the gains from trade.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The infant-industry argument, that a new industry needs shelter until it reaches efficient scale. The other three are arguments for free trade.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The protection never ends. The case rests on the industry growing up, so indefinite protection is a confession that it has not.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A tax on imports. A quantity limit is a quota, a subsidy is a payment, and a ban is an embargo.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It restricts quantity and produces no revenue; the gain goes to holders of the import licence. A tariff raises revenue and also raises the domestic price.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Price up, quantity down. Domestic producers gain, consumers lose more than the producers gain, and the government collects the difference.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An embargo. It is a political instrument as much as an economic one, and its cost falls on everyone who traded with the banned partner.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Selling below cost, usually to clear surplus or to destroy a rival industry. Subsidised exports are related but not identical, and equal pricing at home and abroad is normal trade.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The import supply shrinks, so the domestic price rises towards the protected producer's cost. Whether domestic producers are less efficient is the reason the protection was wanted.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A deliberate official reduction. A market-driven fall is depreciation, and an increase is revaluation or appreciation.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Exports cheaper in foreign currency, imports dearer in naira. Reversing the two is the commonest error in the topic.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Marshall–Lerner condition. If demand is inelastic both ways, the value of imports rises even as their volume falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Worse first, better later: contracts are already signed and buying habits change only gradually, so the first months usually show a deterioration.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Visible goods only. Including services gives the balance on goods and services, and the current account adds income and transfers.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Services are invisible because they cross no border as physical goods. Oil, cocoa and machinery are all visible trade.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Transfers such as remittances are part of the current account. Direct investment, portfolio investment and reserve changes belong to the capital account.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Every transaction is recorded twice, so the two accounts must sum to zero. That does not mean any component is in balance.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Inflows of 12 against a deficit of 9 leave a surplus of ₦3 billion, which adds to reserves. The two figures are not added together.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    More service exports earn foreign exchange without any import content. A stronger currency, higher inflation and more imports all worsen the account.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It must be financed, and financing cannot continue indefinitely. Reserves run out, lenders lose confidence and the currency comes under pressure.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Economic Community of West African States, founded in 1975, which aims at a customs union and eventually a common currency.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Free internal trade with a common external tariff. A common currency and central bank go further than a customs union requires.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A voice in coordination. No single member sets the world price, and no cartel can guarantee a share of exports.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Export prices over import prices, usually indexed. A fall means a country must sell more to buy the same imports — a deterioration.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Export prices have fallen relative to import prices, so more must be sold to buy the same quantity of imports.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Crude oil is a physical good crossing the border. Banking, tourism and insurance are services, which are invisible trade.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The price of one currency in another. It is a price, and like any price it is determined by supply and demand unless it is fixed.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A stronger currency makes imports cheaper, which tends to worsen the trade balance, and makes exports dearer abroad, which tends to reduce them.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A quota restricts quantity without a tax, which is what makes it a non-tariff barrier. The other three are fiscal instruments and therefore tariff-type measures.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zero transport costs and immobile factors are two of its standard assumptions — which is precisely why the real world deviates from the simple prediction.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    To cover imports and support the currency. Using reserves for recurrent spending is the classic route to a balance of payments crisis.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Direct investment is a capital inflow. Export proceeds, remittances and shipping earnings all sit in the current account.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Domestic inflation raises costs and prices, undoing the price advantage the devaluation created, which is why devaluation without demand management rarely works twice.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Removing tariffs, quotas and other restrictions so that goods and services move more freely; it is the opposite of protection.
  ]

]

